---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, VIC2026, formatDateLong } from "../lib/site.mjs";
import { findCampaignEvent } from "../../../scripts/lib/campaign-events.mjs";

const event = findCampaignEvent("peoples-forum-2026-11-17");
const room = [
  { name: "Jess Wilson (Liberal)", pct: 39 },
  { name: "Ellen Sandell (Greens)", pct: 25 },
  { name: "Warren Pickering (One Nation)", pct: 16 },
  { name: "Ben Carroll (Labor)", pct: 7 },
  { name: "Undecided", pct: 13 },
];
const updated = "2026-11-18";

const schema = {
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "Leaders' debate: the People's Forum, 17 November 2026",
  datePublished: updated,
  dateModified: updated,
  url: `${SITE.url}${VIC2026}/leaders-debate`,
  isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE.url}/#website` },
  about: { "@type": "Event", name: "People's Forum (Victorian leaders' debate)", startDate: event.date },
};
---

<Base
  title="Victorian leaders' debate 2026: the People's Forum"
  description="Sourced summary of the 17 November 2026 Victorian leaders' debate (News24/Herald Sun People's Forum): the audience room poll as reported, key exchanges with attributed quotes, and voting dates. No scoring, no forecasts."
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Victoria 2026", href: VIC2026 },
    { name: "Leaders' debate", href: `${VIC2026}/leaders-debate` },
  ]}
>
  <article class="wrap wrap-narrow section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Victoria 2026 · Campaign events · Updated <time datetime={updated}>{formatDateLong(updated)}</time></p>
    <h1>Leaders' debate: the People's Forum, 17 November 2026</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Victoria's four main party leaders met on Tuesday 17 November at the People's Forum, a
      90-minute televised debate hosted by News24 and the Herald Sun at the Sofitel in Melbourne.
      News24 chief news anchor Kieran Gilbert moderated, and 100 undecided voters put questions to
      Premier Ben Carroll (Labor), Opposition Leader Jess Wilson (Liberal), Greens leader Ellen
      Sandell and One Nation Victorian leader Warren Pickering.
    </p>
    <p>
      AAP reported it was the only confirmed leaders' debate before the 28 November election.
      Guardian Australia reported there will be no second debate because Carroll and Wilson did
      not agree terms for one on free-to-air television.
    </p>

    <h2>Audience poll</h2>
    <p>
      As the 100 undecided voters left, they were asked who had won. News24 published the results,
      and ABC News, Guardian Australia and AAP reported them:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Leader</th><th scope="col" class="num">Share of room</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {room.map((r) => (<tr><td>{r.name}</td><td class="num">{r.pct}%</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="section-aside">
      This was a single studio audience of 100 people, not a representative survey of Victorian
      voters. It is not entered in the tracker's <a href={`${VIC2026}/polls`}>poll ledger or average</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Self-selecting online readers' polls gave different results. The Age reported that its own
      readers' poll, with more than 5,500 votes cast in the half-hour after the debate, had Carroll on
      48%, Wilson and Sandell on 23% each and Pickering on 6%. Online polls like this are open to
      anyone and are not representative.
    </p>

    <h2>Key exchanges</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Former premiers.</strong> Wilson challenged Carroll to say the names of his
      predecessors, Daniel Andrews and Jacinta Allan. Guardian Australia reported she told him they
      were "not Voldemort", and AAP quoted her as saying "You can't even say their names." Carroll
      replied, "I can say their names, just like you can say Jeff Kennett's name" (AAP). Wilson
      answered: "Jeff Kennett." Guardian Australia reported that Carroll did not say Andrews' or
      Allan's names during the exchange.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Debt and costings.</strong> According to AAP, the first audience question was on state
      debt, which is projected to approach $200 billion by 2030. Carroll challenged Wilson's promise
      of a cash surplus by 2032, citing research he said showed it would need $40 billion in cuts.
      Wilson replied: "It's just desperate lies and a scare campaign." She later told him: "I won't
      be lectured by you or your government on costings when you have racked up $200bn worth of debt
      in this state" (Guardian Australia).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Donations, treaty and protest.</strong> Guardian Australia reported that Sandell
      criticised all three other parties over their backers: Labor over gambling-industry donations,
      the Liberals over money from property developers, and One Nation over links to Gina Rinehart.
      She and Wilson disagreed on treaty, renewable energy and public service cuts, and on
      pro-Palestinian protests in Melbourne's CBD. Pickering agreed with Sandell that politicians
      should not decide who gets to protest.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Crime.</strong> Pickering said Victoria resembled "Gotham City" because of rising
      crime. He said: "I don't give a damn if our prisons are full. You've broken the law. You're
      going to jail. They're bringing in a supermax in New South Wales. That's where you're bloody
      going" (Guardian Australia).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Net zero.</strong> Pickering pressed Wilson on the legislated net zero targets. The Age
      reported that Wilson confirmed a Coalition government would not change them, while One Nation
      wants them scrapped.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Suburban Rail Loop.</strong> Wilson left open scrapping the project after a review,
      once a Coalition government had "visibility" of the signed contracts. Asked by Gilbert whether
      the Coalition would be willing to scrap it, she said: "That's exactly what the review is for"
      (Guardian Australia; The Age).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Labor's record.</strong> Carroll acknowledged that Labor had made mistakes in 12 years
      in government. He pointed to what he described as its record: 121 new schools, record NAPLAN
      results, the highest-paid teachers in the country and 17,000 nurses. He also cited his decision
      to call a royal commission into corruption in the construction sector (Guardian Australia; AAP).
      These are the Premier's claims as reported. The tracker has not checked them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Trust.</strong> Guardian Australia reported that the only rousing applause of the night
      went to Billy, a schoolteacher in the audience, who asked how the leaders would rebuild trust
      when "nobody believes a word you say any more".
    </p>

    <h2>Polling context</h2>
    <p>
      AAP's debate coverage cited the latest RedBridge/Accent poll for the Australian Financial
      Review (fieldwork 3–10 November, n=1,008). It had primary votes of Coalition 31%, Labor 23%,
      One Nation 22% and Greens 15%, with a reported two-party-preferred result of Coalition 57,
      Labor 43. That poll is already in the tracker. All published polls are on the
      <a href={`${VIC2026}/polls`}>Victorian polls page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Voting</h2>
    <p>
      Early voting opened at 9am on Wednesday 18 November and runs until 6pm on Friday 27 November.
      Election day is Saturday 28 November (Victorian Electoral Commission). AAP reported that the VEC
      expects almost two-thirds of voters to vote early. See
      <a href={`${VIC2026}/voting`}>how to vote in Victoria 2026</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {event.sources.map((s) => (
        <li>
          {s.publisher}, <a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title ?? s.url}</a>
          {s.published && <> ({formatDateLong(s.published)})</>}
        </li>
      ))}
      <li>
        Victorian Electoral Commission,
        <a href="https://www.vec.vic.gov.au/voting/2026-state-election" rel="noopener">2026 state election</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="section-aside">
      The Election Tracker reports what was said and published. It does not score debates and makes
      no forecasts. Corrections: open a GitHub issue.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .num {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .sources {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
</style>
